'''''Reconquista!''''' ({{Langx|bl|¡Reconquista!|4=Reconquest!}}) is a [[W:Right-wing politics|right-wing]], traditionalist, and [[W:Counter-revolution|counter-revolutionary]] political movement within the [[Baliscan post-revolution exodus|Baliscan diaspora]]. Formally established in exile during the early 1920s, Reconquista! seeks the overthrow of the [[W:Plurinationalism|plurinational]] Baliscan state and the restoration of a social, political, and economic order based on traditional hierarchies, religious principles, and an exclusionary vision of Baliscan national identity. The movement synthesizes elements of [[Pan-Andalusianism]], [[W:National syndicalism|national syndicalism]], and historically, [[W:National catholicism|National Catholicism]].

== History ==

=== Origins in exile (1920s–1930s) ===
Reconquista! was formally established in the early 1920s, primarily in [[Brazil]] and [[Argentina]], where many dispossessed [[Cataloccitan aristocracy|elites]], [[W:Large landowner|landowners]], [[W:Financier|financiers]], and other prominent [[W:Capitalist|capitalist]] from across Balisca had sought refuge. These individuals, many of whom were [[Castilian Baliscans|Castilian]] or [[Aragonese Baliscans|Aragonese]] elites who had benefited from the pre-revolutionary [[Cosmocazo|oligarchic political system]], were radicalized by the Revolution's dismantling of their economic power, the collectivization of agrarian areas, and the abolition of traditional social structures. The movement also attracted numerous religious conservatives, predominantly [[W:Catholicism|Catholic]] traditionalists, incensed by the Revolution's violent [[W:Anti-clericalism|anti-clericalism]] and moves towards the [[W:Laicism|total separation]] of religion from public life.

The specific ideological blend of Reconquista! emerged from this shared opposition. Exiled intellectuals and former political figures sought to articulate a coherent counter-revolutionary philosophy that would provide a unifying vision for the diverse diaspora. They drew inspiration from contemporary conservative and authoritarian movements in Fosia and Abyala, adapting them to the specific context of their perceived loss in Balisca.

== Ideology and aims ==
The ideology of Reconquista! is a syncretic blend of traditionalist currents, defined by its stark opposition to the core tenets of the revolutionary Baliscan state. Most importantly, Reconquista! explicitly rejects [[W:Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialism]], radical [[W:Decentralization|decentralization]], [[W:Polyculturalism|polyculturalism]], [[W:Laicism|laicism]], and [[W:Worker control|worker control]] as implemented in Balisca. It views these as destructive forces that undermined a natural, hierarchical order. Unlike the inclusive, polyethnic, and [[W:Postnationalism|postnationalist]] identity promoted by the revolution, Reconquista! champions an exclusive vision of Baliscan identity, often manifesting as [[W:Pan-Hispanism|Pan-Hispanism]], emphasizing a shared [[W:Hispanic culture|Hispanic]] heritage, portraying it as the "authentic" foundation of Balisca. This aspect often downplays or excludes the significant historical contributions of diverse [[Afro-Baliscans|African]], [[W:Maghrebis|Maghrebi]], [[W:Levant|Levantine]], [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]], and [[Asian Baliscans|Asian]] communities to the Baliscan identity.

Reconquista! supports the [[W:Socialization (economics)|socialization]] of the economy by taking the [[Trade unions in Balisca|trade union]] as the basis for working life in a way that the people involved in the management of enterprises and the economy through trade unions organized by productive sectors. It rejects [[W:Capitalism|capitalism]] and promotes the strengthening of [[Social protection in Balisca|social protection]]. They argue that the ownership of the means of production should lead to the [[W:Collectivization|collectivization]], but not as advocated by [[W:Marxism|Marxists]] in the [[W:State ownership|ownership]] of the state, but in the sense of [[W:Common ownership|collective ownership]] of the neighborhood, community or unions. They include the right to a decent life, which not only leads them to reject the [[W:Death penalty|death penalty]], [[Abortion in Balisca|abortion]], torture, racism and [[W:Voluntary euthanasia|euthanasia]], but also [[W:Discrimination|discrimination]] based on [[W:Religion|religion]], [[W:Gender|gender]], and [[W:Sexual orientation|sexual orientation]].

They believe that political participation should not be seen only as a right but also a duty of citizens and therefore political participation should not be limited to political parties, but should also actively extended to civic associations, unions and companies.

=== Activities and impact ===
The movement publishes a newspaper, ''La Verdad Baliscana'' (The Baliscan Truth), and produces other propaganda in exile that strongly condemns the post-revolution regime. They often depict contemporary Baliscan society as decadent, [[W:Libertine|libertine]], extremist, anti-religious and a threat to regional and global stability, and have historically sought to influence public opinion in host countries.

In the immediate aftermath of [[Baliscan post-revolution exodus|the exodus]], Reconquista! actively lobbied foreign governments, particularly conservative or right-wing countries in [[W:Europe|Europe]] (e.g., [[W:Fascist Italy|Fascist Italy]] and [[W:Francoist Spain|Francoist Spain]] in the inter-war and post-war periods) and other [[W:Latin America|Latin American]] countries, in an attempt to politically and economically isolate the revolutionary regime taking shape in Balisca. While the [[Right-wing insurgency in Balisca|right-wing insurgency]] within Balisca itself persisted immediately after the Revolution, Reconquista!'s direct material support for it was often limited. 

During the [[W:Cold War|Cold War]], the party aligned with the [[United States]] and the [[W:Western Bloc|Western Bloc]], attempting to position themselves as the legitimate voice of the Baliscan people and appeal to shared anti-socialist and anti-anarchist sentiments. This included support for [[W:Operation Condor|Operation Condor]] and lobbying for [[W:United States involvement in regime change in Latin America|American support for an attempt at regime change]]. Prominent members of Reconquista! supported Argentina in the [[South Atlantic War]] in the hopes of further destabilizing Baliscan society during the [[Special Period]], and derailing the reforms of the [[Low Impact Initiative]]. With the success of the Special Period-era reforms, the resiliency of Balisca's economic and social model, and the end of the Cold War, Reconquista!'s once substantial influence has significantly waned. It has been further undermined with the rise of the [[W:Internet|internet]] and digital [[W:Mass communication|mass communications]], which has largely discredited its longstanding messaging in the Western world. 

In the present, its activities have become primarily focused on maintaining its ideological purity, preserving an idealized memory of pre-revolutionary Balisca, and serving as a political and social anchor for conservative segments of [[Baliscan diaspora|the diaspora]]. Its influence within Balisca itself has remained effectively nonexistent, and is largely non-viable within the mainstream [[Politics of Balisca|political system]], viewed by both mainstream society and the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal administration]] as a fundamentally discriminatory, reactionary and "anti-constitutional" threat.

